=== FINDING 1: `./configure --disable-rseq && make` does not build ===
PRE-EXISTING, not caused by Phase 4.  Section 1a shows the failure with the
current umem_rseq.h; section 1b shows the identical failure after restoring
the pre-Phase-4 umem_rseq.h (git show 5c7fd76:umem_rseq.h), so the extern "C"
guard fix is not the cause.

Cause: test/stress/repro_naive_reload_race.c and
test/stress/repro_rseq_trailing_store.c are in noinst_PROGRAMS
unconditionally, but both use umem_rseq_cache_t and umem_rseq_* entry points
that exist only when UMEM_RSEQ_AVAILABLE is defined.  Neither file has an
availability guard (test/unit/test_rseq_fastpath.c and
test/bench/bench_cache_rseq.c do).  With rseq auto-detected on (every Linux
host with linux/rseq.h) the breakage is invisible; --disable-rseq exposes it.

Fixed by gating both targets on ENABLE_RSEQ in Makefile.am.

=== FINDING 2: `make check` is FLAKY (~40% failure in a default build) ===
test/debugger/test_inspect_e2e.sh, one of the 8 TESTS entries, failed 2 of 5
consecutive runs in a DEFAULT build on the same binary (rc = 0,0,1,0,1).
Always the same assertion:

  [2/8] live findleaks --json (parse + assert counts)
  AssertionError: expected 2 cached, got 1

The script asserts cached_skipped == 2, derived from test_inspect_live.c's
100-iteration alloc/free(32) churn loop.  How many of those 32-byte buffers
are still sitting in a magazine at inspection time is not deterministic: it
depends on the loaded/previous magazine split and on whether the
reap/update thread flushed in between.  The assertion encodes one observed
outcome as an invariant.

This is NOT an AVX2 problem.  The same script passed 5/5 under
--enable-avx2 in the second half of this run; the verify_phase4.sh failure
that appeared under --enable-avx2 was this flake landing in that
configuration.  Attributing it to AVX2 would have been wrong.

NOT FIXED HERE: test/debugger/ is outside this workstream's file set.  The
fix is to assert a plausible range (or quiesce the update thread before
sampling), and it belongs with the coordinated TESTS change.

Consequence for anyone reading a "make check 8/8": that result has roughly a
60% per-run probability in a default build, and a 7/8 with this test failing
is most likely this flake rather than the change under test.

