=== FINDING 2, CORRECTED AND QUANTIFIED ===
My first description of this as simply "flaky" was incomplete.  Measured
rates, same host, same script, 12 runs each:

    default build      :  2/12 failures   (intermittent)
    --disable-rseq      : 12/12 failures   (DETERMINISTIC)

Always the identical assertion, and always the same direction:
"expected 2 cached, got 1".

Pre-existing, not caused by Phase 4.  Control: the pre-Phase-4 tree
(configure.ac, Makefile.am and umem_rseq.h restored from 5c7fd76) built
--disable-rseq fails 8/8 the same way (scripts/ec2/prephase4_flake_check.sh).
So --disable-rseq has been reliably failing `make check` for as long as it
has been possible to build it -- which was not possible at 5c7fd76 at all,
since the ungated rseq repros broke that build (FINDING 1).  That is
presumably why nobody saw this: --disable-rseq could not reach `make check`.

Interpretation, stated as far as the evidence goes and no further: the
assertion pins cached_skipped to 2, and how many of test_inspect_live.c's
100 churn-loop buffers remain in a magazine at inspection time is not
fixed by anything in the test.  With rseq compiled out, the allocation path
differs (umem.c's UMEM_RSEQ_AVAILABLE blocks in _umem_cache_alloc /
_umem_cache_free disappear entirely), and the resulting distribution lands
on 1 every time rather than sometimes.  I have NOT isolated which specific
difference produces it, and I am not claiming the count "should" be 2 or 1 --
only that the test asserts a quantity it does not control, and that this is
independent of this workstream's changes.

CONSEQUENCE FOR VERIFICATION, worth stating plainly: scripts/ec2/verify_phase4.sh
initially RETRIED make check when this was the only failure.  That was wrong
and I removed it.  Retrying an intermittent failure until it passes is bad
enough; here it would have converted a 12/12 deterministic failure into a
reported pass on a second attempt that could never succeed -- manufacturing
a green result.  The script now reports this case as KNOWN-PREEXISTING,
counted separately from both PASS and FAIL, and never retries.
